    <section id="sect-00011">
      <title>Data Set K: Anxiety, Caffeine, and Resting Heart Rate — 100 Students</title>
      <para id="para-00032">Data type: SIMULATED — these values were computer-generated for classroom use, not collected from real people or events.</para>
      <para id="para-00033"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-k">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00034">One hundred students completed a test that rates anxiety on a 1 to 5 scale, where 1 means not anxious at all and 5 means very anxious. For each student, average daily caffeine consumption and resting heart rate were also recorded.</para>
      <para id="para-00035">Variables</para>
      <list list-type="bulleted" id="list-00001">
        <item>Anxiety score — whole number from 1 (not anxious at all) to 5 (very anxious)</item>
        <item>Caffeine — average milligrams consumed per day, recorded to the nearest 5 mg (a 12-oz cup of coffee is roughly 95 mg)</item>
        <item>Resting heart rate — beats per minute</item>
      </list>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00012">
      <title>Data Set L: Commute from Home to School — 100 People</title>
      <para id="para-00036">Data type: SIMULATED — these values were computer-generated for classroom use, not collected from real people or events.</para>
      <para id="para-00037"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-l">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00038">For each of 100 people: the one-way distance from home to school (miles), the time the trip takes (minutes), the type of car they drive, and the average gasoline used for the one-way trip (gallons).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00013">
      <title>Data Set M: Survey of 200 College Students — Sex, Sports, Job, GPA</title>
      <para id="para-00039">Data type: SIMULATED — these values were computer-generated for classroom use, not collected from real people or events.</para>
      <para id="para-00040"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-m">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00041">Two hundred college students were surveyed. Each student reported sex, whether they play sports, whether they have a job, and their GPA.</para>
      <para id="para-00042">Variables</para>
      <list list-type="bulleted" id="list-00002">
        <item>Sex — Male or Female (48 male, 152 female)</item>
        <item>Plays sports — Yes or No</item>
        <item>Has a job — Yes or No</item>
        <item>GPA — reported on a 4.00 scale, to two decimal places</item>
      </list>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00014">
      <title>Data Set N: Michael Jordan — Points per Game, Six Championship Seasons</title>
      <para id="para-00043">Data type: COLLECTED — this data was drawn from real, published, or personally logged records.</para>
      <para id="para-00044"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-n">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00045">Each column lists the points Michael Jordan (Chicago Bulls) scored in every game he played that season — all regular-season games first, followed by all playoff games (shaded cells). Jordan played every regular-season game except in 1991–92 (80 of 82) and 1992–93 (78 of 82). The final row gives the number of games (n) in each column.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00015">
      <title>Data Set O: Survey of 300 College Students — Employment, Food Delivery, Gaming, Social Media</title>
      <para id="para-00046">Data type: SIMULATED — these values were computer-generated for classroom use, not collected from real people or events.</para>
      <para id="para-00047"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-o">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00048">Three hundred college students were surveyed about employment, food delivery spending, and screen time.</para>
      <para id="para-00049">Variables</para>
      <list list-type="bulleted" id="list-00003">
        <item>Works — Yes or No</item>
        <item>Work status — Full-time, Part-time, or Not at all (students who answered "No" above are recorded as "Not at all")</item>
        <item>Food delivery — dollars spent on food delivery in a typical week, to the nearest dollar</item>
        <item>Video games — hours per week spent playing video games, to one decimal place</item>
        <item>Social media — hours per week spent on social media, to one decimal place</item>
      </list>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00016">
      <title>Data Set P: SAT Study Habits — 500 High School Students</title>
      <para id="para-00050">Data type: SIMULATED — these values were computer-generated for classroom use, not collected from real people or events.</para>
      <para id="para-00051"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-p">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00052">For each of 500 students: total hours spent studying for the SAT, SAT score (400–1600, rounded to the nearest 10), cumulative GPA (4.0 scale), average hours of sleep per night, and average hours per day on social media.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00017">
      <title>Data Set Q: Song Lengths by Artist — 20 Artists, 240 Releases, 2,846 Songs</title>
      <para id="para-00053">Data type: COLLECTED — this data was drawn from real, published, or personally logged records.</para>
      <para id="para-00054"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-q">Link to Excel Version of Data</link></para>
      <section id="sect-00018">
        <title>Overview</title>
