      <para id="para-00055">A full breakout of the individual song-length data by artist (all 20 artists) is available as Data Set R, which is generated from this data set.</para>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00019">
      <title>Data Set R: Song Lengths by Artist</title>
      <para id="para-00056">Data type: COLLECTED — this data was drawn from real, published, or personally logged records.</para>
      <para id="para-00057"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/stats-excel-data-set-r">Link to Excel Version of Data</link> – The data set is long so only the Excel version is available.</para>
      <para id="para-00058">All artists in table Q have their individual song-lengths broken out for studio albums or EPs.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00020">
      <title>Appendix. Releases Counted, by Artist</title>
      <para id="para-00059">For each artist: the releases whose tracks make up that artist’s column, the inclusion decisions behind them, and the sources consulted.</para>
      <section id="sect-00021">
        <title>Nine Inch Nails — 14 releases, 183 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00060">Releases and track counts: Pretty Hate Machine (1989) — 10; Broken (1992, EP) — 8; The Downward Spiral (1994) — 14; The Fragile (1999) — 23; With Teeth (2005) — 13; Year Zero (2007) — 16; Ghosts I-IV (2008) — 36; The Slip (2008) — 10; Hesitation Marks (2013) — 14; Not the Actual Events (2016, EP) — 5; Add Violence (2017, EP) — 5; Bad Witch (2018) — 6; Ghosts V: Together (2020) — 8; Ghosts VI: Locusts (2020) — 15.</para>
      <para id="para-00061">Notes: 14 studio albums/EPs. Broken counted as 8 songs (6 standard + hidden tracks 98/99; the 91 one-second silent tracks excluded). Excluded: remix albums (Fixed, Further Down the Spiral), live releases, compilations, soundtracks.</para>
      <para id="para-00062">Sources: Wikipedia: Nine Inch Nails discography and individual album pages (en, plus de/fr/it/pt/ja editions where the English duration column did not render)</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00022">
        <title>Selena Gomez — 8 releases, 91 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00063">Releases and track counts: Kiss &amp; Tell (Selena Gomez &amp; the Scene) (2009) — 13; A Year Without Rain (Selena Gomez &amp; the Scene) (2010) — 10; When the Sun Goes Down (Selena Gomez &amp; the Scene) (2011) — 12; Stars Dance (2013) — 11; Revival (2015) — 11; Rare (2020) — 13; Revelacion (2021, EP) — 7; I Said I Love You First (with benny blanco) (2025) — 14.</para>
      <para id="para-00064">Notes: 3 Selena Gomez &amp; the Scene albums, 3 solo albums, 1 EP, and the 2025 joint album with benny blanco. Excluded: For You (compilation), soundtrack EPs, all deluxe/bonus tracks.</para>
      <para id="para-00065">Sources: Wikipedia: Selena Gomez discography and album pages; Discogs standard-edition pressings and Last.fm for per-track durations</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00023">
        <title>Taylor Swift — 13 releases, 178 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00066">Releases and track counts: Taylor Swift (2006) — 11; Beautiful Eyes (2008, EP) — 6; Fearless (2008) — 13; Speak Now (2010) — 14; Red (2012) — 16; 1989 (2014) — 13; Reputation (2017) — 15; Lover (2019) — 18; Folklore (2020) — 16; Evermore (2020) — 15; Midnights (2022) — 13; The Tortured Poets Department (2024) — 16; The Life of a Showgirl (2025) — 12.</para>
      <para id="para-00067">Notes: 12 original studio albums plus the Beautiful Eyes EP. Excluded per your instruction: all (Taylor's Version) re-recordings and vault tracks; also excluded deluxe/platinum/3am/Anthology bonus tracks, live and holiday releases.</para>
      <para id="para-00068">Sources: Wikipedia: Taylor Swift albums discography and individual album pages (track listings read from the live pages)</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00024">
        <title>Led Zeppelin — 9 releases, 81 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00069">Releases and track counts: Led Zeppelin (1969) — 9; Led Zeppelin II (1969) — 9; Led Zeppelin III (1970) — 10; Led Zeppelin IV (1971) — 8; Houses of the Holy (1973) — 8; Physical Graffiti (1975) — 15; Presence (1976) — 7; In Through the Out Door (1979) — 7; Coda (1982) — 8.</para>
      <para id="para-00070">Notes: All 9 studio albums, original track listings. Excluded: live albums (The Song Remains the Same, How the West Was Won), compilations, and reissue companion discs.</para>
      <para id="para-00071">Sources: Wikipedia: Led Zeppelin discography and album pages (track order/counts/totals); Apple Music/iTunes catalogue for per-track durations, cross-checked against Wikipedia album totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00025">
        <title>Rihanna — 8 releases, 100 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00072">Releases and track counts: Music of the Sun (2005) — 13; A Girl like Me (2006) — 13; Good Girl Gone Bad (2007) — 12; Rated R (2009) — 13; Loud (2010) — 11; Talk That Talk (2011) — 11; Unapologetic (2012) — 14; Anti (2016) — 13.</para>
      <para id="para-00073">Notes: All 8 studio albums, standard editions (Good Girl Gone Bad is the original 2007 issue, not Reloaded). No studio album has followed Anti as of August 2026. Excluded: compilations, remix albums, reissues, soundtracks, all deluxe/international bonus tracks.</para>
      <para id="para-00074">Sources: Wikipedia: Rihanna albums discography and album pages; Apple Music/iTunes catalogue for per-track durations, cross-checked against Wikipedia totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00026">
        <title>Pink Floyd — 15 releases, 154 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00075">Releases and track counts: The Piper at the Gates of Dawn (1967) — 11; A Saucerful of Secrets (1968) — 7; More (1969) — 13; Ummagumma (studio disc only) (1969) — 5; Atom Heart Mother (1970) — 5; Meddle (1971) — 6; Obscured by Clouds (1972) — 10; The Dark Side of the Moon (1973) — 10; Wish You Were Here (1975) — 5; Animals (1977) — 5; The Wall (1979) — 26; The Final Cut (1983) — 12; A Momentary Lapse of Reason (1987) — 10; The Division Bell (1994) — 11; The Endless River (2014) — 18.</para>
      <para id="para-00076">Notes: All 15 studio albums (the studio soundtracks More and Obscured by Clouds included). Ummagumma contributes its 5 studio-disc tracks only; its live disc is excluded. A Momentary Lapse of Reason uses the original 1987 10-track listing.</para>
      <para id="para-00077">Sources: Wikipedia: Pink Floyd discography, album pages and song infoboxes (en, plus de/ru/pl editions where the English duration column did not render); validated against English infobox album totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00027">
        <title>Beyonce — 8 releases, 120 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00078">Releases and track counts: Dangerously in Love (2003) — 15; B'Day (2006) — 13; I Am... Sasha Fierce (2008) — 11; 4 (2011) — 12; Beyonce (2013) — 14; Lemonade (2016) — 12; Renaissance (2022) — 16; Cowboy Carter (2024) — 27.</para>
      <para id="para-00079">Notes: 8 solo studio albums, standard editions. Excluded: Destiny's Child, the Carters' Everything Is Love, live albums, compilations, and the curated soundtrack The Lion King: The Gift. The 2013 album counts its 14 songs, not the accompanying videos.</para>
      <para id="para-00080">Sources: Wikipedia: Beyonce albums discography and album pages (standard-edition track tables read from the live pages); AllMusic and Album of the Year used as cross-checks</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00028">
        <title>Queen — 15 releases, 172 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00081">Releases and track counts: Queen (1973) — 10; Queen II (1974) — 11; Sheer Heart Attack (1974) — 13; A Night at the Opera (1975) — 12; A Day at the Races (1976) — 10; News of the World (1977) — 11; Jazz (1978) — 13; The Game (1980) — 10; Flash Gordon (1980) — 18; Hot Space (1982) — 11; The Works (1984) — 9; A Kind of Magic (1986) — 9; The Miracle (1989) — 10; Innuendo (1991) — 12; Made in Heaven (1995) — 13.</para>
      <para id="para-00082">Notes: All 15 studio albums, original UK track listings (Flash Gordon and the posthumous Made in Heaven included, as Wikipedia counts both). Excluded: live albums, compilations, CD-reissue bonus tracks, and all Queen + Paul Rodgers / Queen + Adam Lambert releases.</para>
      <para id="para-00083">Sources: Wikipedia: Queen discography and album pages (en, plus de/it/es/ru editions where the English duration column did not render); validated against printed album totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00029">
        <title>Madonna — 15 releases, 175 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00084">Releases and track counts: Madonna (1983) — 8; Like a Virgin (1984) — 9; True Blue (1986) — 9; Like a Prayer (1989) — 11; Erotica (1992) — 14; Bedtime Stories (1994) — 11; Ray of Light (1998) — 13; Music (2000) — 10; American Life (2003) — 11; Confessions on a Dance Floor (2005) — 12; Hard Candy (2008) — 12; MDNA (2012) — 12; Rebel Heart (2015) — 14; Madame X (2019) — 13; Confessions II (2026) — 16.</para>
      <para id="para-00085">Notes: 15 studio albums, standard editions, including Confessions II (July 2026). Excluded as soundtracks: I'm Breathless and Evita. Also excluded: live albums, compilations, remix albums, and all bonus/reissue tracks.</para>
      <para id="para-00086">Sources: Wikipedia: Madonna albums discography and album pages (track listings, counts, editions); Apple Music/iTunes catalogue for per-track durations</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00030">
        <title>Garth Brooks — 14 releases, 160 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00087">Releases and track counts: Garth Brooks (1989) — 11; No Fences (1990) — 10; Ropin' the Wind (1991) — 10; Beyond the Season (1992) — 11; The Chase (1992) — 10; In Pieces (1993) — 10; Fresh Horses (1995) — 10; Sevens (1997) — 14; Garth Brooks &amp; the Magic of Christmas (1999) — 14; Scarecrow (2001) — 12; Man Against Machine (2014) — 14; Gunslinger (2016) — 10; Fun (2020) — 14; Time Traveler (2023) — 10.</para>
      <para id="para-00088">Notes: 14 solo studio albums, including the two Christmas studio albums. Excluded: In the Life of Chris Gaines (credited to the Chris Gaines persona), the duets album Christmas Together with Trisha Yearwood, live albums (Double Live, Triple Live) and box-set compilations.</para>
      <para id="para-00089">Sources: Wikipedia: Garth Brooks discography (album list and the 1989 debut); AllMusic and Discogs original pressings for per-track durations</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00031">
        <title>Run the Jewels — 4 releases, 46 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00090">Releases and track counts: Run the Jewels (2013) — 10; Run the Jewels 2 (2014) — 11; Run the Jewels 3 (2016) — 14; RTJ4 (2020) — 11.</para>
      <para id="para-00091">Notes: All 4 studio albums, standard editions. Excluded: the remix albums Meow the Jewels and RTJ Cu4tro, instrumental versions, and deluxe bonus tracks. No fifth studio album as of August 2026.</para>
      <para id="para-00092">Sources: Wikipedia: Run the Jewels discography and album pages; AllMusic for per-track durations, cross-checked against Wikipedia album totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00032">
        <title>Shakira — 12 releases, 145 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00093">Releases and track counts: Magia (1991) — 9; Peligro (1993) — 10; Pies Descalzos (1995) — 11; Donde Estan los Ladrones? (1998) — 11; Laundry Service (2001) — 13; Fijacion Oral, Vol. 1 (2005) — 12; Oral Fixation, Vol. 2 (2005) — 11; She Wolf (2009) — 12; Sale el Sol (2010) — 15; Shakira (2014) — 12; El Dorado (2017) — 13; Las Mujeres Ya No Lloran (2024) — 16.</para>
      <para id="para-00094">Notes: All 12 studio albums, including the early Colombian releases Magia and Peligro, for which Wikipedia now carries full liner-note track lengths. Excluded: live albums (MTV Unplugged, Live from Paris), compilations, remix albums, and deluxe/regional bonus tracks.</para>
      <para id="para-00095">Sources: Wikipedia: Shakira discography and album pages (en and es editions); each album's sum checked against Wikipedia's stated total</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00033">
        <title>Van Halen — 12 releases, 126 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00096">Releases and track counts: Van Halen (1978) — 11; Van Halen II (1979) — 10; Women and Children First (1980) — 9; Fair Warning (1981) — 9; Diver Down (1982) — 12; 1984 (1984) — 9; 5150 (1986) — 9; OU812 (1988) — 9; For Unlawful Carnal Knowledge (1991) — 11; Balance (1995) — 12; Van Halen III (1998) — 12; A Different Kind of Truth (2012) — 13.</para>
      <para id="para-00097">Notes: All 12 studio albums, standard editions. Instrumentals and short intros (Eruption, Spanish Fly, Cathedral, Intruder, and so on) are counted as tracks because they are numbered songs on the albums. Excluded: live albums, compilations, and format-specific bonus tracks.</para>
      <para id="para-00098">Sources: Wikipedia: Van Halen discography and album pages (album lists, editions, totals); TheAudioDB catalogue for per-track durations, cross-checked against Wikipedia totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00034">
        <title>Radiohead — 11 releases, 115 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00099">Releases and track counts: Pablo Honey (1993) — 12; My Iron Lung (1994, EP) — 8; The Bends (1995) — 12; OK Computer (1997) — 12; Airbag/How Am I Driving? (1998, EP) — 7; Kid A (2000) — 10; Amnesiac (2001) — 11; Hail to the Thief (2003) — 14; In Rainbows (2007) — 10; The King of Limbs (2011) — 8; A Moon Shaped Pool (2016) — 11.</para>
      <para id="para-00100">Notes: All 9 studio albums plus the two full-length EPs Wikipedia lists as official releases. Excluded: the In Rainbows Disk 2 bonus disc, live albums, compilations, remix albums, reissue bonus discs, and the shorter promo EPs (Drill, Itch, COM LAG).</para>
      <para id="para-00101">Sources: Wikipedia: Radiohead discography and album pages (en, plus fr/de/it/pt/ru/ja editions where the English duration column did not render); sums checked against English infobox totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00035">
        <title>Olivia Rodrigo — 3 releases, 36 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00102">Releases and track counts: Sour (2021) — 11; Guts (2023) — 12; You Seem Pretty Sad for a Girl So in Love (2026) — 13.</para>
      <para id="para-00103">Notes: All 3 studio albums, standard editions (GUTS (spilled) deluxe tracks and the 2026 album's bonus track excluded). She has no standalone studio EP: her EPs are High School Musical: The Musical: The Series soundtracks or a live release.</para>
      <para id="para-00104">Sources: Wikipedia: Olivia Rodrigo discography and album pages; AllMusic, Qobuz and Last.fm for per-track durations, cross-checked against one another</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00036">
        <title>Nirvana — 4 releases, 39 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00105">Releases and track counts: Bleach (1989) — 11; Blew (1989, EP) — 4; Nevermind (1991) — 12; In Utero (1993) — 12.</para>
      <para id="para-00106">Notes: All 3 studio albums plus the Blew EP. Bleach uses the original 1989 Sub Pop 11-track listing. The hidden tracks Endless, Nameless (Nevermind) and Gallons of Rubbing Alcohol (In Utero) are excluded, as both fall outside the standard track listing. Excluded: Incesticide (compilation), Hormoaning (tour-only BBC sessions and B-sides), and all live albums.</para>
      <para id="para-00107">Sources: Wikipedia: Nirvana discography and album pages (classifications, counts, totals); Discogs original-era pressings for per-track durations, cross-checked against Wikipedia totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00037">
        <title>Bob Marley and the Wailers — 13 releases, 134 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00108">Releases and track counts: The Wailing Wailers (1965) — 12; Soul Rebels (1970) — 12; Soul Revolution Part II (1971) — 12; The Best of the Wailers (1971) — 10; Catch a Fire (1973) — 9; Burnin' (1973) — 10; Natty Dread (1974) — 9; Rastaman Vibration (1976) — 10; Exodus (1977) — 10; Kaya (1978) — 10; Survival (1979) — 10; Uprising (1980) — 10; Confrontation (1983) — 10.</para>
      <para id="para-00109">Notes: All 13 releases Wikipedia classifies as studio albums, including the early Studio One and Lee Perry era records. The Best of the Wailers (1971) is included because, despite its title, it is a studio album of new recordings rather than a compilation. Excluded: Live!, Babylon by Bus, Legend and all other live, compilation and posthumous remix releases.</para>
      <para id="para-00110">Sources: Wikipedia: Bob Marley and the Wailers discography and album pages; AllMusic for albums whose Wikipedia duration column did not render; Confrontation and Exodus sums match Wikipedia's stated totals exactly</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00038">
        <title>Tim McGraw — 16 releases, 204 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00111">Releases and track counts: Tim McGraw (1993) — 10; Not a Moment Too Soon (1994) — 10; All I Want (1995) — 12; Everywhere (1997) — 11; A Place in the Sun (1999) — 14; Set This Circus Down (2001) — 14; Tim McGraw and the Dancehall Doctors (2002) — 15; Live Like You Were Dying (2004) — 16; Let It Go (2007) — 14; Southern Voice (2009) — 12; Emotional Traffic (2012) — 12; Two Lanes of Freedom (2013) — 11; Sundown Heaven Town (2014) — 13; Damn Country Music (2015) — 11; Here on Earth (2020) — 16; Standing Room Only (2023) — 13.</para>
      <para id="para-00112">Notes: All 16 solo studio albums, standard editions. Excluded: The Rest of Our Life (joint album with Faith Hill), the Poet's Resume EP, live albums and greatest-hits compilations.</para>
      <para id="para-00113">Sources: Wikipedia: Tim McGraw discography and album pages (album list, editions, totals); AllMusic for per-track durations, cross-checked against Wikipedia infobox totals</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00039">
        <title>Wu-Tang Clan — 6 releases, 93 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00114">Releases and track counts: Enter the Wu-Tang (36 Chambers) (1993) — 12; Wu-Tang Forever (1997) — 27; The W (2000) — 13; Iron Flag (2001) — 12; 8 Diagrams (2007) — 14; A Better Tomorrow (2014) — 15.</para>
      <para id="para-00115">Notes: All 6 group studio albums, standard editions (Wu-Tang Forever counts both discs of the US release). Excluded: solo member albums, The Saga Continues (classified as a compilation), the single-copy Once Upon a Time in Shaolin, and all Wu-Tang collective compilations.</para>
      <para id="para-00116">Sources: Wikipedia: Wu-Tang Clan discography and album pages (track tables read from the live pages); Discogs used to resolve the Iron Flag / Da Glock track-length discrepancy</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00040">
        <title>Prince — 40 releases, 494 songs</title>
      <para id="para-00117">Releases and track counts: For You (1978) — 9; Prince (1979) — 9; Dirty Mind (1980) — 8; Controversy (1981) — 8; 1999 (1982) — 11; Purple Rain (1984) — 9; Around the World in a Day (1985) — 9; Parade (1986) — 12; Sign o' the Times (1987) — 16; Lovesexy (1988) — 9; Batman (1989) — 9; Graffiti Bridge (1990) — 17; Diamonds and Pearls (1991) — 13; Love Symbol Album (1992) — 18; Come (1994) — 10; The Black Album (1994) — 8; The Gold Experience (1995) — 18; Chaos and Disorder (1996) — 11; Emancipation (1996) — 36; Crystal Ball (1998) — 30; The Truth (1998) — 12; Newpower Soul (1998) — 11; Rave Un2 the Joy Fantastic (1999) — 18; The Rainbow Children (2001) — 15; One Nite Alone... (2002) — 10; Xpectation (2003) — 9; N.E.W.S. (2003) — 4; Musicology (2004) — 12; The Chocolate Invasion (2004) — 10; The Slaughterhouse (2004) — 10; 3121 (2006) — 12; Planet Earth (2007) — 10; Lotusflow3r (2009) — 12; MPLSound (2009) — 9; 20Ten (2010) — 10; Art Official Age (2014) — 13; Plectrumelectrum (2014) — 12; Hit n Run Phase One (2015) — 11; Hit n Run Phase Two (2015) — 12; Welcome 2 America (2021) — 12.</para>
      <para id="para-00118">Notes: 40 studio albums, standard original editions, following Wikipedia's studio-album table. Segues, interludes and hidden tracks are counted as songs. Crystal Ball contributes its 3 discs of new material (the box set's NPG Orchestra disc Kamasutra is excluded); The Truth is counted separately, as Wikipedia does. Welcome 2 America is the one posthumous release Wikipedia classifies as a studio album; archival compilations such as Originals are excluded, as are live albums, the Bria Valente album Elixer bundled with the 2009 set, and all reissue bonus material.</para>
      <para id="para-00119">Sources: Wikipedia: Prince albums discography and individual album pages (album list, ordinals, track counts, editions); PrinceVault original-pressing listings and the Apple Music/iTunes catalogue for per-track durations; Discogs for Plectrumelectrum; each album's sum cross-checked against published album totals</para>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00041">
      <title>References</title>
      <para id="para-00120">The sources below apply only to the data sets above that were actually collected — drawn from real, published, or personally logged records (Data Sets B, C, J, N, Q). The remaining data sets (A, D, E, F, G, H, I, K, L, M, O, P) are computer-simulated.</para>
      <section id="sect-00042">
        <title>Data Sets B and N: Jalen Brunson and Michael Jordan (NBA Points per Game)</title>
      <para id="para-00121">Set 1 (Michael Jordan):</para>
      <para id="para-00122">Land of Basketball. “Michael Jordan Game by Game Stats.” Seasons 1990–91 through 1992–93 and 1995–96 through 1997–98. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.landofbasketball.com/nba_players_game_logs/1991/michael_jordan.htm (and the corresponding pages for 1992, 1993, 1996, 1997, and 1998).</para>
      <para id="para-00123">StatMuse. “Michael Jordan Game Log.” Used as an independent cross-check of the Land of Basketball game logs. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.statmuse.com/nba.</para>
      <para id="para-00124">MichaelJordansWorld.com. “1997–98 Game-by-Game Log.” Additional cross-check for the 1997–98 season. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.michaeljordansworld.com/gamebygame/gamelog19971998.htm.</para>
      <para id="para-00125">Set 2 (Jalen Brunson):</para>
      <para id="para-00126">StatMuse. “Jalen Brunson Points in Each Game, 2025–26 Regular Season” and “Jalen Brunson Points in Each Game, 2026 Playoffs.” Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.statmuse.com/nba/ask/jalen-brunson-points-in-each-game-2025-26-regular-season and https://www.statmuse.com/nba/ask/jalen-brunson-points-in-each-game-2026-playoffs.</para>
      <para id="para-00127">Land of Basketball. “Jalen Brunson Game by Game Stats — 2025–26 Season.” Used as an independent cross-check. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.landofbasketball.com/nba_players_game_logs/2026/jalen_brunson_full.htm.</para>
      <para id="para-00128">Verification note: every season’s list was checked against the player’s published season totals — Jordan regular seasons: 2,580 pts/82 g (1990–91), 2,404/80 (1991–92), 2,541/78 (1992–93), 2,491/82 (1995–96), 2,431/82 (1996–97), 2,357/82 (1997–98); Jordan playoffs: 529/17, 759/22, 666/19, 552/18, 590/19, 680/21; Brunson 2025–26: 1,927 pts/74 g regular season and 539/19 playoffs.</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00043">
        <title>Data Set Q: Song Lengths by Artist</title>
      <para id="para-00129">Wikipedia. Artist discography pages and individual album articles, English edition (with German, French, Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, Russian, Polish and Japanese editions consulted where the English track-listing tables did not render a duration column). Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://en.wikipedia.org/. Wikipedia was the authority for which releases count as studio albums or EPs, for standard-edition track listings, for track counts, and for stated album running times.</para>
      <para id="para-00130">AllMusic. Album credits and track-length listings. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.allmusic.com/.</para>
      <para id="para-00131">Discogs. Original-pressing release listings. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.discogs.com/.</para>
      <para id="para-00132">Apple Inc. iTunes / Apple Music catalogue lookup service (per-track durations at millisecond precision). Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://itunes.apple.com/ and https://music.apple.com/.</para>
      <para id="para-00133">PrinceVault. Album pages documenting original-pressing track times. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://princevault.com/.</para>
      <para id="para-00134">TheAudioDB. Album and track database. Retrieved August 2, 2026, from https://www.theaudiodb.com/.</para>
      <para id="para-00135">Last.fm, Qobuz, Spotify and Album of the Year. Used as secondary cross-checks on individual track durations. Retrieved August 2, 2026.</para>
      <para id="para-00136">A note on precision. Published song lengths differ slightly between sources: liner notes, original vinyl pressings, CD issues, remasters and streaming files can disagree by one to three seconds on a given track, because fades and lead-in silence are counted differently. Wherever a per-track figure came from a source other than Wikipedia, that album’s summed running time was checked against the album total published on Wikipedia, and the two agreed to within a few seconds. Differences of that size do not affect any statistic a reader is likely to compute from these data, but they are the reason two references may print 3:44 and 3:46 for the same song.</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00044">
        <title>Data Sets J and C: Lap Times and Stock Prices</title>
      <para id="para-00137">Data Set J (Lap Times): recorded directly from Terri Vogel's personal racing log book; no external published citation applies — this is primary-source data.</para>
      <para id="para-00138">Data compiled by Jay R. Ritter of University of Florida using data from Securities Data Co. and Bloomberg.</para>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>